WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.370
NARRATOR: It calls to
mind events that occurred

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:06.906
more than a century
ago, which means

00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:11.911
the key to the present
dilemma may lie in the past.

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:14.214
DAN HUBER: Understanding
patterns in historical attacks

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.350
can also help us to understand
patterns in current attacks.

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:22.021
NARRATOR: So experts are
turning their attention back,

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:27.160
all the way back to the
very first significant shark

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:30.363
attack spike in US history--

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:36.236
1916, the Jersey Shore,
when terror reigned.

00:00:39.372 --> 00:00:41.908
MICHAEL CAPUZZO: The 1916 shark
attacks on the New Jersey Shore

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:45.712
are the greatest shark
attack mystery of all time.

00:00:45.779 --> 00:00:47.647
And really, the movie
"Jaws" and the book

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.783
would not have been
possible without it.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.151
NARRATOR: Though
people at the time

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:54.621
believed sharks to be
harmless, five beachgoers

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.356
are bitten in just two weeks.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:03.563
Four lose their lives,
causing panic up

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.266
and down the Eastern seaboard.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:09.002
For years, experts have
studied these attacks

00:01:09.068 --> 00:01:12.105
and struggled to
explain what happened.

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:14.674
But there is one parallel
between the very first

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:17.944
fatal attack in 1916
and one of the more

00:01:18.011 --> 00:01:23.149
recent attacks that might prove
to be more than a coincidence.

00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:28.054
They both happened at
similar times of day.

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:34.127
In 1916, the first fatal victim
was bitten around 5:00 PM,

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:36.196
just like Ida Parker.

00:01:36.262 --> 00:01:39.833
IDA PARKER: It was about
5:00 in the afternoon.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:43.236
NARRATOR: And according to
shark expert Dr. Dan Huber,

00:01:43.303 --> 00:01:45.705
this may not be mere chance.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.774
DAN HUBER: Recent research
has shown that sharks can use

00:01:47.841 --> 00:01:49.309
the angle of the
sun in order to give

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:51.845
them a predatory advantage.

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:54.914
NARRATOR: If a shark is hunting
when the sun is high overhead,

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:59.786
the water is brightly lit
and potential prey can easily

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:01.621
see the shark's approach.

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:04.057
DAN HUBER: It's going
to illuminate the shark.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:05.692
Perhaps so much so
that it's really

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:08.528
not worth their time to hunt
during the middle of the day.

00:02:08.595 --> 00:02:10.763
NARRATOR: But when the
sun is lower in the sky,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:14.133
like it is around 5:00
PM, everything changes.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:20.573
To explain, Huber sets
up a demonstration.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.808
DAN HUBER: Here we
have a small lure,

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:24.010
which represents the shark.

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:26.379
Next, we have a GoPro
camera, which represents

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.181
the point of view of the prey.

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:32.418
And last, we have the light,
which represents the sun.

00:02:32.485 --> 00:02:35.288
NARRATOR: When the sun
is close to the horizon,

00:02:35.355 --> 00:02:39.158
sharks approach prey using
a very clever method.

00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.895
DAN HUBER: They'll position
the sun behind themselves

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:47.000
so if the prey were to look
where the shark is coming from,

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.402
it's going to be
blinded by the sun.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:51.371
As you can see from
the footage, the scene

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:55.041
gets completely washed
out, making it harder

00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:58.311
to see the oncoming predator.

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:02.382
NARRATOR: This may be key to
understanding the attacks.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:05.351
Because due in part
to this camouflage,

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:08.655
sharks often hunt at two
particular times of day.

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:09.956
DAN HUBER: Sharks
will be more actively

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:11.324
hunting during dawn and dusk.

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:17.664
NARRATOR: As a result,
experts the world over advise

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:20.967
beachgoers to avoid
the water both early

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:27.006
and late in the day when they
may run into a hunting shark.

00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:32.111
So were beachgoers in 1916
and today simply swimming

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:35.014
during these dangerous times?

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.951
Huber examines
the data and finds

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:41.321
that, while most of the
victims in both spikes

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:43.790
were bitten during
the afternoon,

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:47.660
none were bitten
at dawn or dusk.

00:03:47.727 --> 00:03:51.631
DAN HUBER: So the idea that this
would have caused the attacks

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:52.699
simply isn't supported.

